# Hecke acts scalarly on boundary symbols at primes congruent to one

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.boundary_hecke_scalar_at_one`
(`72403d14-b31b-48f8-9651-b731d7f1e8b2`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `d4d40c8d-78e3-4164-ab2e-07668c93275e`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-07 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N>0$, $n\ge0$, and let $\Phi$ be a $\Gamma_1(N)$-equivariant homogeneous degree-$n$ boundary
datum. For every prime $\ell\equiv1\pmod N$, the explicit adjugate-normalized Hecke operator
satisfies

$$T_\ell(\partial\Phi)=(1+\ell^{n+1})\partial\Phi.$$

This is an identity on all boundary cochains, with no eigenvector or nebentype hypothesis. It uses
the convention $(\partial\Phi)(x,y)=\Phi(y)-\Phi(x)$. The coefficient of the second Hecke term is 1.

## Explanation of the source proof

Write the boundary cochain as

$$
\partial\Phi(x,y)=\Phi(y)-\Phi(x).
$$

For a cusp $z$, let $S_\ell\Phi(z)$ denote the sum of the $\ell+1$ adjugate-normalized cusp
transforms occurring in the definition of $T_\ell$.  The primitive-vector calculation, isolated as
`boundary_hecke_cusp_sum_at_one`, gives

$$
S_\ell\Phi(z)=(1+\ell^{n+1})\Phi(z)
$$

when $\ell$ is prime and $\ell\equiv1\pmod N$.  Indeed, among the standard Hecke representatives
exactly one sends a primitive cusp representative to a vector of content $\ell$; the other $\ell$
terms have content one, and homogeneity of degree $n$ produces the stated scalar.

It remains only to commute the Hecke sum with the endpoint difference.  Expanding `primeHecke`,
`slash`, and `boundaryCochain`, additivity of the polynomial action and `Finset.sum_sub_distrib`
identify the left side at $(x,y)$ with

$$
S_\ell\Phi(y)-S_\ell\Phi(x).
$$

Applying the cusp-wise identity at $y$ and $x$, then distributing scalar multiplication over
subtraction, gives

$$
(1+\ell^{n+1})(\Phi(y)-\Phi(x)),
$$

which is the required equality of cochains.

